      'Safe sleep guidance isn\'t just cautious advice — it\'s grounded in one of the more successful public health interventions of the past several decades, and knowing the actual scale of that success is worth understanding, not just the rules themselves.',
      'The "Back to Sleep" campaign, renamed "Safe to Sleep" in 2012, launched in 1994, teaching parents to place babies on their backs to sleep instead of on their stomachs, which had been common practice before. Between 1994 and 1999 alone, the overall US SIDS rate dropped by more than 50%, falling from about 1.2 deaths per 1,000 live births to roughly 0.4. That\'s an enormous decline attributable substantially to one specific behavior change, which is part of why back-sleeping remains the single most emphasized piece of current guidance.',
      'The AAP\'s most recent full update, published in 2022, lays out the current evidence-based core: back sleeping for every sleep until age 1, a firm and flat (not inclined) sleep surface with a fitted sheet and nothing else, and room-sharing without bed-sharing. It also recommends breastfeeding, avoiding exposure to smoking, alcohol and drugs, staying current on routine immunizations and offering a pacifier at sleep times once breastfeeding is established — a broader set of protective factors than just sleep position and space alone.',
      'Room-sharing specifically has real case-control research behind it: a combined analysis of five large studies covering 1,472 SIDS cases and 4,679 controls found that in the very lowest-risk scenario — neither parent smoking, baby under 3 months, breastfed, no other risk factors — bed-sharing still carried about five times the risk of room-sharing, even though the absolute risk in both situations was low (roughly 0.08 per 1,000 for room-sharing versus 0.23 per 1,000 for bed-sharing in that scenario). Risk climbs substantially higher with smoking, alcohol or drug use in the household, which is why bed-sharing is specifically discouraged rather than treated as a personal preference with no real difference either way.',
