      'Guidance on starting solids has been reshaped by a small number of genuinely landmark studies over the past decade, not just accumulated folk wisdom — it\'s worth knowing which parts of current advice come from a real turning point in the research.',
      'Readiness is more about developmental signs than a specific age, though most babies are ready somewhere between 4 and 6 months: sitting with minimal support, good head and neck control, loss of the tongue-thrust reflex (they stop automatically pushing food back out with their tongue), and genuine interest in what you\'re eating.',
      'Baby-led weaning (offering soft, appropriately sized whole foods from the start) and traditional spoon-feeding with purees are both legitimate, research-supported approaches, and the comparative evidence is more nuanced than either camp\'s advocates tend to suggest. Systematic reviews have found choking incidents don\'t differ significantly between the two methods when food is prepared appropriately, and growth outcomes are mixed across studies rather than clearly favoring one approach. The real, consistently documented concern specific to baby-led weaning is a higher risk of iron deficiency if iron-rich foods aren\'t deliberately prioritized, identified in a widely cited 2012 review by researchers Cameron, Heath and Taylor — a solvable problem with intentional food choice, not a reason to avoid the approach.',
      'The biggest actual shift in guidance came from a single landmark trial: the LEAP study (Learning Early About Peanut Allergy), led by researcher George Du Toit and published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2015. Researchers randomized 640 infants at high risk for peanut allergy (due to severe eczema, egg allergy or both) to either regularly consume peanut protein starting between 4 and 11 months, or avoid peanut entirely until age 5. Early introduction produced a substantial reduction in peanut allergy development, and the protective effect held up even after a follow-up year of avoidance in the LEAP-On extension study. That single trial is why guidance flipped from delaying common allergens to introducing them early — it wasn\'t a gradual accumulation of opinion, it was one well-designed study changing the standard of care.',
