    title: 'Wake Windows: The Real Sleep Science Behind Them',
    category: 'Sleep',
    readMinutes: 6,
    summary: 'A decades-old model of sleep regulation, and the actual brain chemical behind why staying up too long backfires.',
    body: [
      'Wake windows aren\'t just a parenting-blog concept — they\'re a practical application of a real, decades-old model of how sleep is actually regulated in the brain, which is worth knowing since it explains why the concept works and why it\'s only ever an estimate.',
      'Sleep researcher Alexander Borbély proposed the two-process model of sleep regulation in 1982, and it remains a foundational framework in sleep science today. It describes sleep as governed by two interacting systems: a homeostatic process, often called "Process S," that builds sleep pressure the longer you\'re awake and discharges it during sleep, and a circadian process, "Process C," that gates when sleep is biologically favored, independent of how tired you actually are.',
      'The molecular basis of that homeostatic sleep pressure is adenosine, a chemical that steadily accumulates in the brain during wakefulness and promotes sleepiness — it\'s the same molecule caffeine works by blocking, which is why caffeine keeps you feeling alert rather than actually reducing how tired you are. In infants, this system is still immature, and wake windows start short and lengthen gradually as the nervous system matures over the first couple of years. Naps discharge that built-up pressure, effectively resetting the clock for the next stretch of wakefulness.',
      'The specific minute-by-minute ranges on wake-window charts and apps aren\'t drawn from one single landmark study — they reflect broad clinical and pediatric sleep consensus built on top of this real underlying mechanism. Because there\'s genuine individual variation in how quickly any given baby\'s sleep pressure actually builds, watching your specific baby\'s cues (rubbing eyes, staring off, fussiness, yawning) alongside a general age-based range is more reliable than treating a chart as an exact number to hit.',
      'Why overtiredness backfires has a real physiological basis, consistent with broader sleep-deprivation research: staying awake well past the point your body was ready to sleep functions as a genuine stressor, and prolonged wakefulness is associated with activation of the same stress-hormone pathways — cortisol and adrenaline — that keep you alert under pressure generally. That heightened arousal state is part of why an overtired baby often fights sleep harder and wakes more, not less, which is the opposite of what exhausted parents expect and exactly backwards from what feels intuitive in the moment.',
      'If bedtime or naps are consistently a fight, the wake window is one of the first things worth checking — sometimes shortening it by even 15 to 20 minutes makes a noticeable difference, since it\'s working with the actual sleep-pressure mechanism rather than against it.',
      'None of this means chasing an exact number obsessively. The research behind the concept describes a real biological process, but one with meaningful individual variation — the chart is a starting estimate, and your baby\'s actual cues are the more reliable signal layered on top of it.',
    ],
