    id: 'ppd-vs-baby-blues',
    emoji: '🌦️',
    title: 'Postpartum Depression vs. Baby Blues: The Actual Difference',
    category: 'Postpartum',
    readMinutes: 7,
    summary: 'Different cause, different timeline, different threshold for concern — and a real connection between the two worth knowing about.',
    body: [
      '"Baby blues" and "postpartum depression" get used almost interchangeably in casual conversation, but they\'re clinically distinct — different cause, different timeline, and a different threshold for concern — and knowing which one you\'re actually experiencing changes what "this is normal, it\'ll pass" should and shouldn\'t apply to.',
      'Baby blues has a specific, well-understood hormonal mechanism: estrogen and progesterone, both dramatically elevated throughout pregnancy, plummet to near pre-pregnancy levels within about 48 hours of delivery. That crash disrupts serotonin and dopamine activity and removes progesterone\'s calming effect on GABA activity in the brain — a real neurochemical event, not just "hormones" as a vague catch-all. How common it is depends heavily on the study and population: a narrative review found reported rates ranging from roughly 14% to 76% across different countries and methodologies, with a pooled estimate around 39% and rates in some Asian countries reported as high as 85% — wide enough variation that any single flat number you see quoted should be taken as a rough guide, not a precise figure.',
      'What\'s more consistent is the timeline: baby blues typically starts 2 to 5 days after delivery and resolves within about two weeks on its own, without treatment. Postpartum depression is different on both counts — it can begin any time in the first year, not just right after birth, and by clinical definition it persists beyond two weeks rather than fading on its own.',
      'The clearer differentiator is function, not just mood. Baby blues involves real tearfulness, mood swings and irritability, but it generally doesn\'t stop you from caring for your baby or functioning day to day. PPD does — persistent sadness or hopelessness that interferes with bonding, sleep beyond what a newborn already disrupts, appetite or your ability to function are markers that something has moved past the blues.',
      'Blues and PPD aren\'t entirely separate categories, though, and that connection matters. A large French cohort study following 3,310 women found postpartum blues — especially when severe — was a significant predictor of later postpartum depression, both early-onset (within 8 weeks) and later-onset. That doesn\'t mean blues will turn into PPD for most people; it means severe blues is worth actually tracking past the two-week mark rather than just assuming it\'ll resolve on schedule.',
      'The same study found several factors that compound risk when present together: a personal or family history of depression or anxiety; childhood trauma; a stressful pregnancy; a difficult birth or delivery complications; and having a first baby. Each additional factor added meaningfully to the overall risk, rather than any single one determining the outcome alone.',
      'In practice, that means it\'s worth actually noting when symptoms started and whether they\'re easing up as the two-week mark approaches, rather than just hoping. The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale is a quick, validated way to check if you\'re unsure, and if symptoms are severe from the start or aren\'t improving by two weeks, that\'s worth a call to your provider rather than more waiting.',
      'Where to start if you\'re not sure: Postpartum Support International, 1-800-944-4773 (postpartum.net), for support and to help sort out what you\'re experiencing — not just for full-blown PPD, but for anyone unsure which side of this line they\'re on.',
    ],
