    id: 'helping-siblings-adjust',
    emoji: '👶',
    title: 'Helping Siblings Adjust to a New Baby',
    category: 'Behavior',
    readMinutes: 7,
    summary: 'The classic research finding isn\'t "sibling rivalry" — it\'s that jealousy and real affection show up together, not as a competition.',
    body: [
      'How an older child responds to a new sibling has real developmental research behind it, and the classic finding is more nuanced than "sibling rivalry": both jealousy and genuine affection tend to show up together, not as a competition where one wins.',
      'The foundational study here is Judy Dunn and Carol Kendrick\'s observational research, which followed families with firstborns between 18 months and 3.5 years old for the first 14 months after a new baby arrived. They found that most firstborns did become more demanding, more prone to acting out and more clingy and tearful in the weeks right after the birth. But the same children, often within the same days, also cuddled the baby, tried to help take care of it, and showed real concern when it cried. Both reactions were normal and simultaneous, not sequential stages to get through.',
      'A 2012 review in Psychological Bulletin by researcher Brenda Volling found that behavioral changes in the first six months after a sibling\'s birth — tantrums, regression in skills like toileting, sleep disruption, increased anxiety — are common and developmentally typical responses to a genuinely large change, not evidence of a behavior problem to correct. The research specifically found that the quality of the caregiver\'s response during this period functions as a buffer, shaping how the adjustment goes more than the initial behavior itself does.',
      'Some of what reads as jealousy is really just close monitoring of a real change: research has found firstborns who paid closer attention to interactions between their mother and the new baby were more likely to be described by parents as acting out — which suggests the "bad behavior" is often a child accurately tracking a real shift in attention, not a character flaw or a sign of poor parenting.',
      'What predicts a good long-term sibling relationship isn\'t the absence of conflict in this period — some conflict is close to universal — it\'s the amount of positive interaction alongside it. Research consistently finds that positivity in the relationship is a stronger predictor of later adjustment than conflict is. In practice, that points at two concrete levers: protecting some one-on-one time with the older child specifically, even in small amounts, and including them in age-appropriate caretaking — handing you a diaper, "helping" soothe, choosing an outfit — rather than only asking them to be patient and share.',
      'Preparing before the birth — reading books about becoming a sibling, talking concretely about what will change and what won\'t, letting them help set up the nursery — is standard, reasonable practice even though it\'s less rigorously studied than the postpartum adjustment period itself. It doesn\'t guarantee a smooth transition, but it gives a child a frame for a change that\'s otherwise happening entirely around and to them.',
      'There\'s real individual variation here, and that\'s worth expecting rather than treating as a red flag: some children move through this with real disruption, others show barely a ripple, and both are within the range the research describes as normal. There isn\'t one correct timeline to hit.',
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: 'Dunn & Kendrick — classic sibling relationships research, summarized', url: 'https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/health-and-medicine/sibling-relationships' },
      { label: 'Volling, "Family Transitions Following the Birth of a Sibling" — Psychological Bulletin (2012)', url: 'https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/volling-lab/wp-content/uploads/sites/362/2019/02/Volling-2012-Family-transitions-after-birth-of-sibling-Psych-Bull.pdf' },
      { label: '"Understanding the Transition to Siblinghood" — developmental psychopathology perspective', url: 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5596879/' },
    ],
